│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ balanced_sa_polish                                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current annealing phase spends 70% of proposals on direct deletion. After the initial │
│                          │ erosion, most uniformly chosen cells are not adjacent to the outside zero component, so   │
│                          │ these proposals are rejected immediately and do not help reshape difficult bottlenecks.   │
│                          │ Use the broader random-cell / neighboring-color kernel instead: it naturally supplies     │
│                          │ deletion, zero restoration, and ward-to-ward recoloring moves. This preserves enough      │
│                          │ restoration and recoloring moves for topology changes, while still allowing deletion      │
│                          │ whenever it is locally feasible.                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Also reserve the last part of the time budget for monotone erosion. Annealing is useful   │
│                          │ for rearranging the map early, but once it has made useful rearrangements, deterministic  │
│                          │ deletion passes exploit newly exposed removable cells more efficiently and cannot degrade │
│                          │ the incumbent best solution.                                                              │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
