│ patch_name               │ frontier_peeler                                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ This version replaces the expensive general simulated annealing loop with a               │
│                          │ frontier-driven constructive search.                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The main insight is that a newly created zero cell is automatically connected to the      │
│                          │ outside if it is either on the grid boundary or adjacent to an existing zero cell.        │
│                          │ Therefore, deletion candidates can be generated directly from the current zero frontier,  │
│                          │ avoiding most invalid isolated-zero proposals. The program repeatedly peels wards from    │
│                          │ the outside while preserving every required adjacency edge.                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To escape local geometric dead ends, it occasionally performs adjacency-preserving        │
│                          │ nonzero-to-nonzero boundary transfers. These reshape ward boundaries without changing the │
│                          │ score, after which frontier peeling resumes. Connectivity checking is reduced to the      │
│                          │ source ward only: the destination ward is known connected because transfers only use a    │
│                          │ neighboring destination color, and zero expansion is known connected by construction.     │
